d> How do logical operators (&&, ||, ---) evaluate expressions, and what is meant by "short-circuit evaluation"?

The AND && operator evaluates from left to right and stops when it encounters a falsy value:
true && true;   // true
true && false;  // false

The OR || operator stops when it encounters a truthy value:
let username = "";

let displayName = username || "Guest";

console.log(displayName); // Guest

Short-circuit evaluation means JavaScript doesn't evaluate the rest of a logical expression once the result is already determined.
For example:
false && console.log("Hello");

console.log() isn't executed because once JavaScript encounters false, the && expression cannot succeed regardless of the second operand.
Similarly:
true || console.log("Hello");

The console.log() isn't executed because true is already enough to satisfy the OR expression.


