13> Parameters and Rest/Spread: Define the arguments object vs. the "Rest Parameter" (...args) syntax. Which is preferred in modern JavaScript and why?
 
Both the arguments object and the rest parameter (...args) allow a function to work with a variable number of arguments. However, modern JavaScript generally prefers rest parameters.
1. The arguments object
Traditional JavaScript functions automatically have access to an arguments object containing the values passed to the function.
function showNumbers() {
    console.log(arguments);
}

showNumbers(10, 20, 30);

The arguments object contains 10, 20, and 30.
You can access individual arguments using indexes:
function showNumbers() {
    console.log(arguments[0]);
    console.log(arguments[1]);
    console.log(arguments[2]);
}

showNumbers(10, 20, 30);

Output:
10
20
30

However, arguments is array-like, not a real JavaScript array. For example, it has indexes and .length, but it doesn't directly provide all normal array methods.
2. Rest parameter — ...args
Rest parameter syntax collects the remaining arguments into a real array:
function showNumbers(...args) {
    console.log(args);
}

showNumbers(10, 20, 30);

Here:
...args

collects the arguments into:
[10, 20, 30]

Because args is a real array, you can easily use methods such as .map(), .filter(), and .reduce().
For example:
function addNumbers(...numbers) {
    return numbers.reduce((total, number) => total + number, 0);
}

console.log(addNumbers(10, 20, 30));

Output:
60

Rest with regular parameters
You can also combine normal parameters with a rest parameter:
function introduce(name, ...hobbies) {
    console.log(name);
    console.log(hobbies);
}

introduce("Leonard", "Coding", "Gaming", "Reading");

Here:
name    → "Leonard"
hobbies → ["Coding", "Gaming", "Reading"]

The rest parameter must be the last parameter:
function example(name, ...args) {
    // valid
}

Which is preferred?
In modern JavaScript, rest parameters (...args) are generally preferred. They produce a real array, make it explicit that the function accepts a variable number of arguments, work naturally with array methods, and also work with arrow functions.

