18> Block Scope vs. Function Scope: Contrast the scoping behavior of var with let and const. How did the introduction of ES6 change how we handle variables inside if blocks and for loops?

The main difference is that var is function-scoped, while let and const are block-scoped.
A block is code surrounded by curly braces {} such as an if statement or for loop.
var — Function Scope
var does not create a new scope just because it appears inside an if block.
if (true) {
    var message = "Hello";
}

console.log(message);

Output:
Hello

Even though message was declared inside {}, it is still accessible outside the if block.
However, var is scoped to functions:
function greet() {
    var message = "Hello";
}

console.log(message); // ReferenceError

Here, message cannot escape the function.
let and const — Block Scope
let and const respect {} blocks.
if (true) {
    let age = 25;
    const name = "Leonard";

    console.log(age);  // Works
    console.log(name); // Works
}

console.log(age);  // ReferenceError
console.log(name); // ReferenceError

age and name only exist within that block.

What changed with ES6?
Before ES6 (ES2015), JavaScript primarily relied on var, so developers had to be careful because variables declared inside if, for, and other blocks could remain accessible outside those blocks.

