This creates a new block-scoped variable named a. It shadows the global:
let a = 10;

Meanwhile:
let b = 20;

was declared inside outer(), so the if block can access it through the outer scope.
Therefore:
console.log(a + b);

becomes:
30 + 20 = 50

After the if block finishes, the local a = 30 no longer exists because let is block-scoped.
So:
console.log(a);

looks for a inside outer(). There isn't one there, so JavaScript looks outward and finds the global:
let a = 10;

Therefore it prints:
10

Finally:
console.log(c);

prints:
40

This works because c was declared with:
var c = 40;

var is function-scoped, not block-scoped. So although c was declared inside the if block, it belongs to the entire outer() function and remains accessible after the if block.


